# =============================================================================
# Trivy misconfiguration ignores.
# =============================================================================
# Location: ci/trivyignore (moved from the repo root).
# Consumed by .github/scripts/ci-iac-scan.sh:
#   trivy config --ignorefile ci/trivyignore \
#     --skip-dirs "**/examples/**" --skip-dirs "**/localstack/**" \
#     --format json --output <report> infra/terraform
# The verdict is NOT trivy's exit code — `agents.ci.ci_gate scan-gate
# --scanner trivy-config` reads the JSON and fails on any HIGH/CRITICAL that
# survives this file. The accepts below were triaged at that threshold.
#
# ID FORMAT IS LOAD-BEARING. trivy filters a misconfiguration when the ignore
# entry matches its `ID`, its `AVDID`, or one of its `Aliases`
# (pkg/result/filter.go). trivy 0.69.3 reports terraform misconfigurations with
# ID `AWS-0104` and an EMPTY AVDID, and the aliases are tfsec rule names
# ("aws-ec2-no-public-egress-sgr"), not AVD identifiers. The `AVD-`-prefixed IDs
# this file used to carry therefore matched nothing — they were inert. Use the
# bare `AWS-0nnn` form that appears in the report, and verify against
# ci-results/trivy-config-report.json rather than against the AVD website URL.
# =============================================================================
# Each ID below is an ACCEPTED finding for this single-account / single-EC2 /
# single-region architecture. Each maps ONLY to the resources noted, so a
# global ignore loses no real coverage today. These mirror the accepts already
# documented in ci/checkov.yaml and the inline checkov:skip annotations.

# AWS-0104 — Security group rule allows unrestricted egress to 0.0.0.0/0.
# The single EC2 host needs outbound internet on 443/80/53 to reach AWS APIs
# (Bedrock, ECR, SSM), OS/package updates, ACME/Let's Encrypt, and DNS. This is
# the documented residual in docs/_audit/tf_security.md (L-9). Tighter control
# would require AWS Network Firewall + domain allow-listing (out of scope for a
# single-EC2 design). Only the app SG egress rules in modules/network match
# (infra/terraform/modules/network/main.tf, the four
# aws_vpc_security_group_egress_rule blocks), reached from the foundation root.
AWS-0104

# AWS-0132 — S3 bucket does not use a customer-managed KMS key. Matches ONLY
# the ALB access-log bucket (modules/alb_internal, reached from the
# velocityai-alb root). AWS's own ELB access-logging documentation states that
# SSE-S3 (AES256) is the ONLY server-side encryption this bucket supports;
# SSE-KMS makes ELB log delivery fail with access denied. A CMK here would
# break log delivery rather than harden it, so this is a provider constraint,
# not a hardening gap. Already accepted for the equivalent checkov rule via the
# inline `checkov:skip=CKV_AWS_145` annotation on that resource — this entry is
# the trivy half of the SAME reviewed decision, which is why the finding was
# reaching the gate unaccepted.
AWS-0132

# AWS-0136 — SNS topic not encrypted with a customer-managed KMS key. The
# alerts topic carries non-sensitive CloudWatch alarm notifications and uses
# the default AWS-managed SNS encryption. Only the monitoring alerts topic
# matches (modules/monitoring, reached from the app root).
AWS-0136

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT ignored — recorded so the absences are deliberate, not oversights.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AWS-0031 (ECR mutable tags) WAS accepted here on the grounds that a build
# retry needs to re-push the same tag. That accept is withdrawn: the repos now
# default to IMMUTABLE (infra/terraform/modules/ecr) and build retries are
# handled by reusing the existing image's digest instead of re-pushing it
# (.github/workflows/deploy.yml, "Probe ECR for an existing image"). The check
# is intentionally live so that flipping a repo back to MUTABLE fails CI. The
# only AWS-0031 hits today come from infra/terraform/localstack, which pins
# `image_tag_mutability = "MUTABLE"` on purpose for a throwaway local emulator
# and is excluded by --skip-dirs, not by an ignore — so the check still fires
# for shared/ and for the module's own example.
